## Le post

A roof is, in the end, one tile repeated over and over, and the eye spots that repetition fast.

My problem was building a roof where the texture repetition would not show, while staying optimised for a game. So I built the roof of the church in my Louisiana Bayou project. In Houdini, an HDA lays the tiles in offset rows, each row overlapping the one below, with a slider for the number of tiles per row and per column.

Then comes the texture: a 2,048 px sheet holds 36 patterns (6 x 6), and a bit of VEX picks one at random for every tile and moves its UVs to the right cell. Each tile also gets one of four orientations, so 36 patterns x 4 give 144 looks without a bigger texture! (The article shows how I go from one pattern to four with the same texture assignment.)

The whole church runs on three materials, all in 2,048 px, with heightmaps built in Copernicus, Houdini's answer to Substance Designer: a graph that generates textures.

The full breakdown is in the article, and the video is on YouTube in its original format.
